-- title:
--   Supersingular Hecke matrix equals the Brandt matrix at ℓ
-- statement:
--   Let $\kappa$ be an algebraically closed field of prime characteristic $q'$ that is algebraic over $\mathbb{Z}/q'$, let $X_1$ be an elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\kappa$ all of whose $q'$-torsion is trivial, and let $a,b\in\mathbb{Q}$ satisfy `IsDefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt`, i.e. $a<0$, $b<0$ and for every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$ the algebra $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{Q}_v$ is a division ring exactly when $q'\in v$. Let $\Lambda_1$ be a maximal order (a finitely generated $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule containing $1$, closed under multiplication, spanning the algebra over $\mathbb{Q}$, and maximal among such), and let $\theta_1$ be an injective ring homomorphism from the subring of $\operatorname{End}(X_1)$ generated by the rationally represented endomorphisms into $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ with image exactly $\Lambda_1$. Let $N\neq 0$ with $q'\nmid N$, and let $m$ be a unit of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}^{\mathrm{fin}}$ lying in the adelic box of $\Lambda_1$, with $N\,m^{-1}$ in that box, and such that the conjugate order $m\Lambda_1m^{-1}$ (`conjByFiniteIdele`) is again maximal; put $R=\Lambda_1\cap m\Lambda_1m^{-1}$ and assume the relative index of $R$ in $\Lambda_1$ is $N$. Let $e$ be a bijection from the class set of $\mathbb{H}^\times\backslash(\mathbb{H}\otimes\mathbb{A}^{\mathrm{fin}})^\times/\widehat{R}^\times$, where $\widehat{R}^\times$ is the stabiliser of the adelic box of $R$, onto the set `ssPlaces q' N κ` of rational affine geometric places of the modular function field $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\ \kappa\ N$ whose value at the geometric $j$-generator is supersingular. Assume the dictionary clause $he$: for every adelic unit $x$, every elliptic $W/\kappa$ and every nonzero rationally represented $\chi:X_1\to W$, every unit $d$ of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ with $\theta_1$ of the kernel ideal set of $\chi$ equal to the conjugate (star) image of $d\cdot\mathrm{ofFiniteIdele}\,\Lambda_1\,x$, and every pair of rationally represented $\psi:W\to W'$, $\psi':W'\to W$ with $\psi'\circ\psi=N$ and $\psi\circ\psi'=N$ such that the kernel ideal set of $\psi\circ\chi$ corresponds in the same way to $d\cdot\mathrm{ofFiniteIdele}\,\Lambda_1\,(xm)$ and $\ker\psi$ is cyclic of cardinality $N$, the valuation subring of the supersingular place $e([x])$ is the pullback along the inclusion of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\ \kappa\ N$ into the full modular function field of the valuation subring of $\mathrm{moduliPlace}\ \kappa\ N\ W\ (\ker\psi)$. Finally let $\ell$ be a prime with $\ell\neq q'$ and $\ell\nmid N$, and let $h\alpha$, $h\beta$ be integrality witnesses for the two algebra maps $\mathrm{heckeAlphaC}$ and $\mathrm{heckeBetaC}$ from the level-$N$ modular function field into the degeneracy roof at $\ell$. Then for all classes $i,j$ the $(e\,i,e\,j)$ entry of $\mathrm{ssHeckeMatrixC}$ — the sum, over places $W$ of the roof whose $\beta$-restriction is $e\,j$ and whose $\alpha$-restriction is $e\,i$, of the ramification index along $\beta$ times the inertia degree along $\alpha$ — equals the $(i,j)$ entry of $\mathrm{classSetHeckeMatrix}$ for $\widehat{R}^\times$ and the $\ell$-Hecke set of $R$ (the adelic units $h$ with $h$ and $\ell h^{-1}$ in the box of $R$ but $h^{-1}$ and $\ell^{-1}h$ not in it), namely the cardinality of the Hecke incidence set at $(j.\mathrm{out},i)$.
--
--   This is the Eichler–Deuring comparison in matrix form: the degeneracy-roof Hecke correspondence $T_\ell$ on the supersingular points of $X_0(N)$ in characteristic $q'$ is identified with the Brandt matrix of the Eichler order $R=\Lambda_1\cap m\Lambda_1m^{-1}$ at the good prime $\ell$, relative to a bijection $e$ between the class set of $R$ and the supersingular places that is compatible with kernel ideals. It is the Hecke-equivariance half of the supersingular dictionary used to produce a class-set model of the supersingular Hecke module, and is cited in the construction of such a bijection together with commuting Hecke squares.
